th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028+v3-2026-11-02+v3-2026-11-10+v3-2026-11-10+v3-2026-11-10+v3-2026-11-10+type-mix-retire-20261112+type-mix-topup-20261112+type-mix-retire2-20261112+fill-20261112+fill2-20261112","concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Math-II-Linear-Algebra/diagonalization.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Math-II-Linear-Algebra/diagonalization.html","questions":[{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalization_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What do the diagonal entries of $D$ represent in $A=PDP^{-1}$?","options":["Eigenvalues of $A$","Row sums of $A$","The dimensions of eigenspaces","Entries of the first column of $P$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9a48165ecc6f22ecf30f92cf","time_limit":25},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalization_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does diagonalization make the action of a linear map simpler?","options":["In an eigenbasis, it acts as the identity on every axis","In any basis, its matrix is already diagonal","It becomes a rotation of the coordinate axes","In an eigenbasis, it scales each axis separately"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1c49e8544820b0f54eefb43b","time_limit":25},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalization_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $A=P\\operatorname{diag}(2,7)P^{-1}$, what is $A^2$?","options":["$P\\operatorname{diag}(4,49)P^{-1}$","$P\\operatorname{diag}(2,7)P^{-1}$","$P\\operatorname{diag}(4,14)P^{-1}$","$P^2\\operatorname{diag}(2,7)P^{-2}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"552731679d505c44dcfa4b2c","time_limit":20},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalization_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $A=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,3)$, which diagonalization is immediate?","options":["$P=0$, $D=A$","$P=A$, $D=I$","$P=I$, $D=0$","$P=I$, $D=A$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"72ba2d648f3c30ce1d247513","time_limit":25},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalization_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why are eigenvectors placed in the columns of $P$ rather than chosen arbitrarily?","options":["The columns of $P$ are the diagonal entries of $D$.","The equation $AP=PD$ records $Av_i=λ_iv_i$ column by column.","Arbitrary columns always make $P^{-1}AP$ diagonal.","The columns of $P$ must be standard basis vectors only."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a358eb6901da5128a80490ff","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalization_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does diagonalization make computing $A^{k}$ easy?","options":["The inverse of $P$ disappears permanently.","The eigenvalues are replaced by row operations.","The power of every diagonal matrix is zero.","The middle factors $P^{-1}P$ cancel repeatedly."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1bacfbd381b3e8f5e2176720","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalization_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Suppose $P=[v_1\\ v_2]$, $D=\\operatorname{diag}(2,5)$, and $A=PDP^{-1}$. What is $Av_2$?","options":["$0$","$v_1+v_2$","$5v_2$","$2v_2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a0fbac102109f25f5374c814","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalization_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the order of the columns of $P$ matter only together with the order of the diagonal entries of $D$?","options":["Changing the order of columns always destroys invertibility.","Each eigenvector column must be paired with its corresponding eigenvalue.","The diagonal entries of $D$ must be sorted from small to large.","The order never matters, even if the pairings are changed."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"859bac8ee28af538e062f548","time_limit":40},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalization_012_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"An $n\\times n$ matrix $\\boldsymbol{A}$ has independent eigenvectors $\\boldsymbol{p}_1,\\ldots,\\boldsymbol{p}_n$ with $\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{p}_i=\\lambda_i\\boldsymbol{p}_i$. With $\\boldsymbol{P}=[\\boldsymbol{p}_1\\ \\cdots\\ \\boldsymbol{p}_n]$ and $\\boldsymbol{D}=\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1,\\ldots,\\lambda_n)$, which equation always holds?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{A}=\\boldsymbol{P}\\boldsymbol{D}\\boldsymbol{P}^{-1}$","$\\boldsymbol{A}=\\boldsymbol{P}^{-1}\\boldsymbol{D}\\boldsymbol{P}$","$\\boldsymbol{A}\\boldsymbol{P}=\\boldsymbol{D}\\boldsymbol{P}$","$\\boldsymbol{A}=\\boldsymbol{P}\\boldsymbol{D}\\boldsymbol{P}^{\\top}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6104e0f9598718401c7dd017","time_limit":30},{"id":"la_batch18_diagonalization_020_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A claimed diagonalization $\\boldsymbol{A}=\\boldsymbol{P}\\boldsymbol{D}\\boldsymbol{P}^{-1}$ of a $2\\times2$ matrix uses $\\boldsymbol{P}$ with columns $(1,2)^{\\top}$ and $(-2,-4)^{\\top}$, both eigenvectors of $\\boldsymbol{A}$. What is wrong?","options":["The columns are dependent, so $\\boldsymbol{P}$ is not invertible.","The columns are not orthogonal, which diagonalization requires.","Nothing, since both columns are eigenvectors of $\\boldsymbol{A}$.","Nothing, since both columns are nonzero vectors."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b2fd8595d5455c6e58ff9442","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_diagonalization_190372f7_typemix20261112_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol{A}=\\begin{pmatrix}4&1\\\\2&3\\end{pmatrix}$. Which pair $\\boldsymbol{P},\\boldsymbol{D}$ satisfies $\\boldsymbol{A}=\\boldsymbol{P}\\boldsymbol{D}\\boldsymbol{P}^{-1}$?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{P}=\\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\\\1&2\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\boldsymbol{D}=\\operatorname{diag}(5,2)$","$\\boldsymbol{P}=\\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\\\1&2\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\boldsymbol{D}=\\operatorname{diag}(2,5)$","$\\boldsymbol{P}=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\-1&2\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\boldsymbol{D}=\\operatorname{diag}(5,2)$","$\\boldsymbol{P}=\\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\\\1&2\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\boldsymbol{D}=\\operatorname{diag}(4,3)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c935e2f965b8bc63188ee635","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_diagonalization_190372f7_typemix20261112_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"A $2\\times2$ matrix $\\boldsymbol{A}$ has the eigenvector $\\boldsymbol{p}_1=(1,1)^{\\top}$ with eigenvalue $3$ and the eigenvector $\\boldsymbol{p}_2=(1,-1)^{\\top}$ with eigenvalue $1$. What is $\\boldsymbol{A}^2\\boldsymbol{v}$ for $\\boldsymbol{v}=(3,1)^{\\top}$?","options":["$(27,1)^{\\top}$","$(19,17)^{\\top}$","$(28,26)^{\\top}$","$(27,9)^{\\top}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"21182e8b292fae1bae196a25","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_diagonalization_190372f7_typemix20261112_003","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Which statement about diagonalizing $\\boldsymbol{A}=\\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\\\0&2\\end{pmatrix}$ is correct?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{P}=\\boldsymbol{I}$ and $\\boldsymbol{D}=\\operatorname{diag}(2,2)$ work, read off the triangular matrix.","It is diagonalizable, since $(\\lambda-2)^2$ has only real roots.","It is not diagonalizable: $\\ker(\\boldsymbol{A}-2\\boldsymbol{I})$ is only one-dimensional.","It is not diagonalizable, since a repeated eigenvalue always prevents it."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"537f72834116f34b4cae2ab4","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_diagonalization_190372f7_fill220261112_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol{A}=\\boldsymbol{P}\\boldsymbol{D}\\boldsymbol{P}^{-1}$, where $\\boldsymbol{P}=[\\boldsymbol{p}_1\\ \\boldsymbol{p}_2]$ is invertible and $\\boldsymbol{D}=\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2)$ with $\\lambda_1\\neq\\lambda_2$ and both nonzero. Which other pair satisfies $\\boldsymbol{A}=\\boldsymbol{P}'\\boldsymbol{D}'\\boldsymbol{P}'^{-1}$ for every such $\\boldsymbol{P}$ and $\\boldsymbol{D}$?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{P}'=\\boldsymbol{P}$ and $\\boldsymbol{D}'=2\\boldsymbol{D}$","$\\boldsymbol{P}'=[\\boldsymbol{p}_2\\ \\boldsymbol{p}_1]$ and $\\boldsymbol{D}'=\\boldsymbol{D}$","$\\boldsymbol{P}'=\\boldsymbol{P}^{\\top}$ and $\\boldsymbol{D}'=\\boldsymbol{D}$","$\\boldsymbol{P}'=[2\\boldsymbol{p}_1\\ \\ {-\\boldsymbol{p}_2}]$ and $\\boldsymbol{D}'=\\boldsymbol{D}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f833e4e17426dadc6480ffb6","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1b_math_ii_linear_algebra_diagonalization_190372f7_fill220261112_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $\\boldsymbol{A}=\\boldsymbol{P}\\operatorname{diag}(2,-4)\\boldsymbol{P}^{-1}$ for an invertible $2\\times2$ matrix $\\boldsymbol{P}$. Which expression equals $\\boldsymbol{A}^{-1}$?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{P}\\operatorname{diag}\\big(\\tfrac{1}{2},-\\tfrac{1}{4}\\big)\\boldsymbol{P}^{-1}$","$\\boldsymbol{P}^{-1}\\operatorname{diag}\\big(\\tfrac{1}{2},-\\tfrac{1}{4}\\big)\\boldsymbol{P}$","$\\boldsymbol{P}\\operatorname{diag}(-2,4)\\boldsymbol{P}^{-1}$","None: $\\boldsymbol{A}$ is not invertible, as $-4$ is a negative eigenvalue."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c881941ed7fd58ae1075857b","time_limit":30}]}}